From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: CX3576-Z BSP <bsp@cx3576z.local>
Date: Sun, 07 Sep 2026 18:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] rk3576: generic: describe the CX3576-Z recovery key on saradc

The board's recovery button is a resistor divider on SARADC channel 1, and
the generic RK3576 description leaves the controller disabled and declares
no keys.  Without both, `button recovery` finds no button, so the PREBOOT
line that enters RockUSB on a held key resolves to nothing and the only
way out of a bad boot is the maskrom pin.

Describe the divider as an adc-keys node and enable the controller.  The
mainline rockchip-saradc driver refuses to probe without a supply, and the
generic board describes no regulator for the always-on 1.8 V rail, so the
voltage is stated directly.

This was a `printf ... >> $DTSFILE` inside the Dockerfile's build step until
RFCT-345 -- an edit to the vendor source performed by a shell redirection in
the middle of a build, invisible to the series and to review.  It is applied
last because it appends to the file 0005 and 0006 also edit.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [board integration]
---
diff --git a/arch/arm/dts/rk3576-generic.dts b/arch/arm/dts/rk3576-generic.dts
index 1be17f41..3a8367c7 100644
--- a/arch/arm/dts/rk3576-generic.dts
+++ b/arch/arm/dts/rk3576-generic.dts
@@ -172,3 +172,24 @@
 	phy-names = "usb2-phy";
 	status = "okay";
 };
+
+/ {
+	adc-keys {
+		compatible = "adc-keys";
+		io-channels = <&saradc 1>;
+		io-channel-names = "buttons";
+		keyup-threshold-microvolt = <1800000>;
+		poll-interval = <100>;
+		button-recovery {
+			label = "recovery";
+			linux,code = <0x73>;
+			press-threshold-microvolt = <17000>;
+		};
+	};
+};
+
+&saradc {
+	status = "okay";
+	/* Use the always-on 1.8 V rail when no regulator is described. */
+	vdd-microvolts = <1800000>;
+};
